**Dual-write (phase 1).** Legacy `pg-orders-1` stays the source of truth: it receives every write and serves every read. The service writes each order and an `orders_outbox` row in one legacy transaction. `outbox-relay` then copies those rows to orders-v2 in commit order, and `backfill_orders_v2` copies historical orders each night. Nothing reads from orders-v2 yet, so errors there can't reach customers. Cutover can't start until four things hold:

- the backfill is complete;
- the `order-diff` mismatch rate stays under 0.01% for 3 days;
- relay lag p99 stays under 2 seconds for those same 3 days;
- a one-time full-table checksum matches. This is the only check that covers every old order.

**Cutover (phase 2).** Reads move first. `orders.read_from_v2` goes to 1%, 10%, 50% and then 100%, one step a day, while writes still go to both stores. Reads then stay at 100% on orders-v2 for a 48-hour soak. After that, `orders.write_legacy` is turned off and the relay is scaled to zero. Legacy stays up read-only for 30 days before it's decommissioned. The cutover counts as done after 7 clean days and order-history p99 at or under 180 ms.

The caveat that matters is that rollback only works until the write stop. Before it, setting the read flag back to 0 loses nothing, because legacy still has every write. After it, nothing syncs from orders-v2 back to legacy, so falling back would lose every order written since. That's why the write stop needs sign-off from the DBA lead and the commerce director. It's scheduled for 2026-11-30. If it slips, the code freeze pushes it to January, and dual-write keeps running until then.

**Dual-write.** Legacy still handles all reads and writes. Each write also adds a row to `orders_outbox` in the same transaction, and `outbox-relay` copies those rows to orders-v2. Meanwhile, `backfill_orders_v2` copies older orders each night and needs about 11 nights. Nothing reads from orders-v2 yet, so customers can't see any errors there. The phase ends when four things hold:

- the backfill is complete;
- `order-diff` mismatches stay under 0.01% for 3 days;
- relay lag p99 stays under 2 seconds for the same 3 days;
- a one-time full-table checksum matches.

**Cutover.** The flag `orders.read_from_v2` moves reads to orders-v2 in steps of 1%, 10%, 50% and 100%, one step a day. Writes still go to both stores during this ramp. Reads then stay at 100% for a 48-hour soak. Until the soak ends, rolling back just means setting the flag to 0. Turning off `orders.write_legacy` can't be undone, because nothing syncs orders-v2 back to legacy. That's why this step needs sign-off from the DBA lead and the commerce director. After that, legacy stays up read-only for 30 days and is then decommissioned.

One caveat: the timeline has no slack. The four ramp steps and the soak take up all of 11-24 to 11-29. If any ramp step has to be held, the write stop misses 11-30, and the code freeze then pushes it to January.
